#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121022 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we will consider a few more properties of determinants. Let's start with the following important property. \n\n> *The determinant of the product of two square matrices is equal to the product of the determinants.*\n\nSymbolically, if ![](images/34/34058a2a5d1de088feed7d9825ba218394c9a8bb880b046be7b47307cdd37ce1.svg) and ![](images/25/256531b7f0ddb14dd11a59a3019a3361f1607d3fe16fa6d530a461344de8f39e.svg) are both square matrices, then\n\n![](images/e5/e59b47ca5462a066093945e2698404e595a92e9e70a2b8c4f8b2f30df9c9fbf1.svg)\n\nFor instance, suppose that we want to find the determinant of the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) that is defined as follows:\n![](images/9a/9a705ea4bd482cdf83251288108b48ff5bc7354c9d51c1b710b9e734c8a32dea.svg)\n\nTo easily compute ![](images/da/dab9a65c1cba3cd9f271a1910b060e5428813107b3b54945776aab3839003d45.svg), we first compute the determinants of the two matrices and then multiply the results. Also, notice that both matrices in our product are triangular, so their determinants are given by the product of the diagonal entries:\n\n![](images/fa/fa4214541c12b0a761fd7cb17f4efc2f6b4be2c1424094483a3c2f61ab63c0a0.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "5a8abcbc-c41d-5553-8b2e-daa7256d7d34" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3009 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121023 :tutorial/content "Another useful property of determinants is that, for any invertible matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\det(A^{-1}) = \\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)}.\n$$\n\nTo understand why the above property is true, remember that $A \\cdot A^{-1} = I$ for any invertible matrix $A.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot {A}^{-1} & =I \\\\ det(A\\cdot {A}^{-1}) & =det(I) \\\\ det(A)\\cdot det({A}^{-1}) & =1 \\\\ det({A}^{-1}) & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** We can only apply the formula $\\det(A^{-1}) = \\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)}$ if $A$ is invertible, meaning that $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "043b8a4a-8f67-5ae8-a7ae-bbda3fa2bdbe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3010 :tutorial/title "The Determinant of an Inverse Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121024 :tutorial/content "One final important property of the determinant is that it is **linear in each row and column**. For rows, for example, this means \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\,\\,=det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1} & {◻}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]+det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {△}_{1} & {△}_{2} & … & {△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, it's easy to check that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{-2}{\\overset{⏞}{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}} & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{4}+{{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this property also holds for columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det[\\begin{aligned}⋯ & {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{aligned}]=det[\\begin{aligned}⋯ & {◻}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n} & ⋯\\end{aligned}]+det[\\begin{aligned}⋯ & {△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{-2}{\\overset{⏞}{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}} & =det[\\begin{aligned}1+0 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{4}+{{det[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f26cf5f4-fd6d-5502-94d6-4b34bd2568ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3011 :tutorial/title "The Linearity of the Determinant"}]}
